Install a staged kerbside-proxy wheel into the kerbside images.

The kerbside-proxy transition replaces the in-tree Python SPICE proxy
with a compiled Rust binary supervised by "kerbside daemon run". That
binary is not part of the kerbside Python source tree installed by
/kerbside above; it ships in a separate maturin "bin" wheel (name
kerbside-proxy) that lays the compiled binary onto PATH.

Install that wheel when, and only when, one has been staged into the
build tree at kerbside/proxy-wheels/. This lets CI (and local
development) build the kerbside-base image against a proxy compiled from
the checked-out kerbside source, which the daemon then finds on PATH.

Deliberately no PyPI fallback yet. kerbside's default branch still runs
the pure-Python proxy and needs no binary, and kerbside-proxy is not
published on PyPI, so an unconditional "pip install kerbside-proxy"
would break every production image build. Leaving the else branch out
(mirroring the /plugins conditional above) keeps production master
builds byte-for-byte unchanged -- a working Python-proxy container --
until the Rust proxy lands on the default branch and is released to
PyPI, at which point a PyPI fallback can be added here.

